CORRECTION to this commit message.

The subject says "Normalize stored line endings to LF". That describes
only half of what this commit did.

Five files under "* text=auto" (_archive/api.py, _archive/_mt_stats.py,
_archive/_scan_low_pages.py, _archive/export_via_api.py and
_archive/MBh01_translation.md) did go from CRLF blobs to LF blobs, and
still check out as CRLF on Windows. That is normalization.

Eight HTML files under "*.html -text" went the other way: they are now
stored byte-for-byte as they sit in the working copy, which is CRLF.
Verified: scripts/ask.html holds 197 CR-bearing lines in the blob,
matching its working copy exactly.

That is the intended behaviour of -text, not an oversight. These are
export artifacts, and MBh01_translation.html is the file Booksmith
ingests and hashes with sha256_file(). Pinning the bytes is the point:
the same checkout on every platform, so a release identity never moves
because someone opened the file on a different operating system.

Not amended because the commit was no longer HEAD by the time the error
was caught, and rewriting history to fix a message is a worse trade than
carrying a note.
